Good, `description` is a valid option. Now let me think about the exact trigger condition more carefully.

In `fio_keyword_replace`:
```c
while ((s = strstr(opt, kw->word)) != NULL) {
    char *new = calloc(strlen(opt) + 1, 1);
    char *o_org = opt;
    int olen = s - opt;
    int len;

    memcpy(new, opt, olen);
    len = sprintf(new + olen, "%s", kw->replace);

    opt += strlen(kw->word) + olen;
    /* keeps final zero thanks to calloc */
    if (strlen(opt))
        memcpy(new + olen + len, opt, opt - o_org - 1);

    opt = new;
    free(o_org);
    docalc = 1;
}
```

For `description=$ncpusX`:
- `opt = "description=$ncpusX"`, `strlen = 19`
- `s = strstr(opt, "$ncpus")` at position 12
- `olen = 12`
- `new = calloc(20, 1)`
- `memcpy(new, opt, 12)` → copies "description="
- `len = sprintf(new + 12, "%s", kw->replace)` → writes e.g. "8", `len = 1`
- `opt += 6 + 12 = 18` → `opt` now points to `o_org + 18`, which is 'X'
- `strlen(opt) = 1` (the "X" plus null)
- `memcpy(new + 12 + 1, opt, opt - o_org - 1)` = `memcpy(new + 13, opt, 18 - 1 = 17)`

The memcpy reads 17 bytes from `opt` which only has 2 valid bytes (X + null). This reads 15 bytes past the end of the original buffer → heap-buffer-overflow READ.

Also, `new` is 20 bytes. Writing 17 bytes at `new + 13` writes up to `new + 29`, which is 10 bytes past the end → heap-buffer-overflow WRITE.

Wait, but actually, the `memcpy` source is `opt` which points into the original buffer `o_org`. The original buffer was allocated by `strdup` in `__parse_jobs_ini` (line: `opts[num_opts] = strdup(p);`). So the read overflow is on the original `strdup`'d buffer.

